      "worked": "Factorise by grouping:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2y-x-xy^2+y&=xy(x-y)-(x-y)\\\\x^2y-x-xy^2+y&=(x-y)(xy-1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe equation therefore gives $x-y=0$ or $xy-1=0$, so draw $y=x$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ together.\n\nThe line passes through $(0,0)$. The hyperbola lies in quadrants I and III, with asymptotes $x=0$ and $y=0$. Both components pass through $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$. The restriction $x\\ne0$ applies only to the hyperbola; the origin remains on the line.",
